<!-- views/partials/legal-privacy-content.ejs -->
<!-- The real Privacy Policy body. Included by views/legal-document.ejs only
     when documentType === 'PRIVACY'. Every [[REVIEW: ...]] marker below is
     also collected in docs/LEGAL_REVIEW_NOTES.md -- if you resolve one here,
     resolve it there too. -->
<style>
  .privacy-doc { max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 28px 20px 80px; }
  .privacy-doc .logo-row { text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px; }
  .privacy-doc .logo-row img { height: 40px; }
  .privacy-doc h1 { font-size: 24px; letter-spacing: -0.01em; margin: 0 0 6px; text-align: center; }
  .privacy-doc .meta { text-align: center; font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--ink-soft); margin-bottom: 28px; }
  .privacy-doc .intro { font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink); margin-bottom: 22px; }
  .privacy-doc .jump-nav {
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; background: var(--paper-raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 12px; padding: 16px 18px; margin-bottom: 32px;
  }
  .privacy-doc .jump-nav a {
    font-size: 14.5px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--teal-deep); text-decoration: none;
  }
  .privacy-doc section { margin-bottom: 40px; }
  .privacy-doc .section-banner {
    font-size: 11.5px; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--teal-deep); background: var(--teal-wash); display: inline-block;
    padding: 4px 10px; border-radius: 999px; margin-bottom: 10px;
  }
  .privacy-doc h2 { font-size: 20px; margin: 0 0 14px; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
  .privacy-doc h3 { font-size: 15.5px; margin: 22px 0 8px; }
  .privacy-doc p { font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 14px; }
  .privacy-doc ul { margin: 0 0 14px; padding-left: 20px; }
  .privacy-doc li { font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--ink); margin-bottom: 6px; }
  .privacy-doc .review {
    display: inline; background: #DCE7F4; color: #0350BE; padding: 1px 4px;
    border-radius: 4px; font-size: 13.5px; font-weight: 600;
  }
  .privacy-doc hr { border: none; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); margin: 36px 0; }
  .privacy-doc .contact-box {
    background: var(--paper-raised); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 12px;
    padding: 16px 18px; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .privacy-doc .back-top { font-size: 13px; }
</style>

<div class="privacy-doc">
  <div class="logo-row"><img src="/images/receiptap-logo.png" alt="ReceipTap" /></div>
  <h1>ReceipTap Privacy Policy</h1>
  <p class="meta">Version <%= doc.version %> &middot; last updated <%= doc.lastUpdated %></p>

  <p class="intro">
    ReceipTap is the technology behind the NFC tap that gives you a digital
    receipt instead of a paper one. This page explains what happens to
    personal information depending on how you use ReceipTap — as a shopper
    who tapped a receipt, or as a business using ReceipTap at your
    register. It's published by <strong><%= entity.legalName %></strong>
    (<%- entity.registeredAddress || '<span class="review">[[REVIEW: address pending]]</span>' %>),
    and is governed by the laws of <%= entity.governingLaw %>.
  </p>

  <div class="jump-nav">
    <a href="#shopper">I got a receipt from a business →</a>
    <a href="#merchant">I run a business using ReceipTap →</a>
  </div>

  <!-- =================== SHOPPER SECTION (first, per instruction) =================== -->
  <section id="shopper">
    <div class="section-banner">For shoppers</div>
    <h2>If you got a receipt from a business</h2>

    <p>
      Tapping your phone on a ReceipTap puck and viewing your receipt doesn't
      require an account and doesn't collect anything about you personally —
      the receipt just displays. This section only applies once you choose to
      <strong>save</strong> a receipt, by entering your email or signing in
      with Google.
    </p>

    <h3>What we collect, and why</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Your email address</strong> — only if you choose to save a receipt. This is how we know which receipts are yours.</li>
      <li><strong>Your name</strong> — only if you save a receipt by signing in with Google (we get it from your Google account). If you save with just an email, we don't ask for a name.</li>
      <li><strong>The receipts you save</strong> — which business, what you bought, how much, and when — so we can show them to you in one place and let you search or export them later.</li>
      <li><strong>The IP address your device connects from</strong> — only at the moment you tap a consent checkbox (like agreeing to receive marketing emails from a business). This is kept as proof of that specific choice, not to track you.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      We use this to build your receipt wallet, automatically sort your
      purchases into spending categories, and let you download a copy
      whenever you need one — for a return, a warranty claim, or your own
      records.
    </p>

    <h3>The business gets your email too</h3>
    <p>
      When you save a receipt, the business you bought from receives your
      email address as well. They collected it through ReceipTap, but once
      they have it, it's theirs to use — not ours. Whether they email you
      about sales, loyalty offers, or anything else is their decision, and
      following the law when they do is their responsibility (see the
      business section below for what we require of them). If you'd rather a
      specific business not have your email, contact them directly, or see
      "How to unsubscribe" below.
    </p>

    <h3>AI category suggestions</h3>
    <p>
      To sort a purchase into a spending category (like "Food &amp; Drink"
      or "Office Supplies") and flag whether it might be tax-deductible, we
      send the business's name and a short list of what you bought — just
      item names and how many of each, like "2x Coffee, 1x Muffin" — to
      Anthropic, an AI company that provides this feature for us.
    </p>
    <p>
      We do <strong>not</strong> send Anthropic your name, your email, the
      price of anything you bought, or the total amount — only the business
      name and that item list.
    </p>

    <h3>How long we keep your data</h3>
    <p>
      Our policy is to keep your saved receipts, and your account itself, for
      <strong><%= Math.round(retention.SHOPPER_RECEIPT_MONTHS / 12) %> years</strong>,
      and then delete them. We keep them that long on purpose: the Canada
      Revenue Agency expects business records to be kept for six years after
      the end of the tax year they belong to, and US limitation periods can
      run to seven. Deleting sooner would take your receipts away while you
      might still need them. This is our policy and our commitment to you.
      The automated system that enforces it is built and is being finalized
      before we switch it on, so it isn't yet actively removing data on
      this schedule today — if you'd like something deleted sooner, see
      "Getting a copy, deleting your data, or unsubscribing" below.
    </p>

    <h3>Getting a copy, deleting your data, or unsubscribing</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Get a copy:</strong> log into your wallet and export your receipts anytime.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Delete your data:</strong> ask the business directly (they
        can look you up by email and remove your data from their own
        records), or email us at <%= entity.contactEmail %> and we'll help.
        <span class="review">[[REVIEW: today a business can delete your data with them specifically through their own dashboard; a single request to erase your data everywhere across every business you've used ReceipTap at is handled by us by hand, not yet through a self-serve page.]]</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Unsubscribe from marketing:</strong>
        <span class="review">[[REVIEW: ReceipTap itself does not send marketing email today, so there is no unsubscribe link to give here. If a business emails you directly, use their own unsubscribe process. To stop a business from re-adding your email after you've asked them to delete it, email <%= entity.contactEmail %> and we'll record that.]]</span>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Where your data is stored</h3>
    <p>
      Your database records are stored in Canada — Supabase, our database
      provider, hosts them in Montreal. The application server that
      actually runs ReceipTap and processes every request, however, is
      hosted in the United States (Railway, US West region), so your data
      is processed there as well as stored in Canada. A few features
      additionally send part of it outside Canada: Google, to confirm your
      identity if you sign in with Google; Anthropic, for the category
      suggestion described above (using only the business name and item
      list, never your name, email, prices, or total); and Resend, if you
      ever request a password-reset email from us.
      <span class="review">[[REVIEW: confirm whether an additional cross-border transfer safeguard (e.g. standard contractual clauses) is legally required given the application server's US location, beyond disclosing it here.]]</span>
    </p>

    <p class="back-top"><a href="#shopper">↑ Back to top</a></p>
  </section>

  <hr />

  <!-- =================== MERCHANT SECTION =================== -->
  <section id="merchant">
    <div class="section-banner">For businesses</div>
    <h2>If you're a business using ReceipTap</h2>

    <h3>Account data we hold</h3>
    <p>
      We hold your business name, your own name, your email, your login
      (a securely hashed password, or a linked Google account), your
      uploaded logo and receipt design choices, and the connection details
      for the point-of-sale system you connect (Square or Clover) — never
      your customers' card numbers, which don't pass through our systems.
    </p>

    <h3>Billing</h3>
    <p>
      Your payment details are held by Stripe, our payment processor — they
      never reach our own servers. We keep Stripe's own reference IDs for
      your account and subscription so we can manage your billing with you.
    </p>

    <h3>Our role for your customers' data</h3>
    <p>
      When your customers tap and save a receipt, <strong>you</strong>
      decide why their email is being collected and what happens with it —
      that's your call, not ours. ReceipTap acts as a processor: we handle
      that data on your instructions, as your service provider, not as the
      party deciding its purpose. Our
      <a href="/legal/dpa">Data Processing Agreement</a> covers this
      relationship in detail.
    </p>

    <h3>Your responsibilities</h3>
    <p>
      You're responsible for having a lawful reason to email the customers
      whose addresses you collect through ReceipTap, and — if you plan to
      send them promotional or marketing messages — for getting their
      express consent, as required by Canada's Anti-Spam Legislation
      (CASL). ReceipTap's tap-screen checkbox is built to capture and
      record that choice accurately, but making sure you only market to
      customers who said yes, and honoring anyone who said no, is your
      responsibility, not ours.
    </p>

    <h3>Subprocessors</h3>
    <p>We use the following subprocessors to run ReceipTap:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Supabase</strong> — our database, hosted in Canada</li>
      <li><strong>Railway</strong> — hosts and runs the application server itself, in the United States</li>
      <li><strong>Stripe</strong> — payment processing, for your subscription and for affiliate payouts</li>
      <li><strong>Anthropic</strong> — the AI category-suggestion feature described above</li>
      <li><strong>Google</strong> — sign-in verification only (not the "Rate us on Google" link, which is just a URL you provide, not a data integration)</li>
      <li><strong>Square, Clover, Lightspeed, Shopify</strong> — whichever point-of-sale system you connect</li>
      <li><strong>Resend</strong> — password-reset emails</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>If you close your account</h3>
    <p>
      Our policy is to keep your business data for
      <strong><%= retention.DEACTIVATED_MERCHANT_PURGE_DAYS %> days</strong>
      after you close your account, in case you want to come back or need
      help with something, and then remove or de-identify it. This is our
      policy and our commitment; the automated system that enforces it is
      built and is being finalized before we switch it on, so it isn't yet
      actively removing data on this schedule today. If you'd like your
      business data removed sooner, email <%= entity.contactEmail %>.
      Records proving you accepted our Terms, Privacy Policy, and Data
      Processing Agreement are the one exception — we keep those
      permanently, even after your account is closed, since they're our own
      compliance record and not something we're able to delete on request.
    </p>

    <p class="back-top"><a href="#merchant">↑ Back to top</a></p>
  </section>

  <hr />

  <!-- =================== SHARED =================== -->
  <section id="shared">
    <h2>Cookies and local storage</h2>
    <p>
      ReceipTap sets one cookie: a session cookie that keeps you logged in
      (to your wallet, if you're a shopper, or your dashboard, if you're a
      business). It's marked secure in production and expires after 30
      days. We don't set any advertising, tracking, or analytics cookies,
      and we don't use your browser's local storage to store anything about
      you.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you sign in with Google, Google's own sign-in button may set its
      own cookies as part of that — governed by Google's policies, not
      ours. If you're a business and we send you to Stripe to manage
      billing, the same is true of any cookies Stripe's own site sets while
      you're there.
    </p>

    <h2>If something goes wrong</h2>
    <p>
      If a data breach happens that puts your personal information at
      risk, we will notify the people affected and any privacy regulator we
      are required to notify, as required by Canadian law.
      <span class="review">[[REVIEW: confirm the specific notification timeline/thresholds required under PIPEDA and any applicable provincial law before publishing.]]</span>
    </p>

    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      When we change this policy, we'll update the "last updated" date at
      the top of this page.
      <span class="review">[[REVIEW: no mechanism exists today to email every shopper or merchant about a policy change (ReceipTap doesn't send marketing/bulk email). Decide how material changes will be announced — e.g. a dashboard banner — before publishing.]]</span>
    </p>

    <h2>Contact us</h2>
    <div class="contact-box">
      <%= entity.contactRole %><br />
      <%= entity.legalName %><br />
      <%# No registered address configured yet. Print nothing rather than a
          placeholder: a contact block reading "[[REVIEW: address pending]]" was
          being shown to every customer who opened this page. The wrapped
          markers elsewhere still surface it during review. %>
      <% if (entity.registeredAddress) { %><%= entity.registeredAddress %><br /><% } %>
      <a href="mailto:<%= entity.contactEmail %>"><%= entity.contactEmail %></a>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>
